message.content:
You are contributing two self-contained modules to a from-scratch Rust reimplementation (ZERO external crates allowed — std only, edition 2021). Do not add any dependency to Cargo.toml. There is NO network access.

Create exactly two files:

## 1. `/workspace/src/deflate.rs`
A DEFLATE (RFC 1951) raw compressor.

Public API:
```rust
/// Compress `data` into a raw DEFLATE stream (no zlib/gzip wrapper).
pub fn deflate(data: &[u8]) -> Vec<u8>;
/// CRC-32 (IEEE 802.3, as used by ZIP/PNG).
pub fn crc32(data: &[u8]) -> u32;
```
Requirements:
- Must produce output that real inflaters accept (python `zlib.decompress(out, -15)` must round-trip byte-exactly for arbitrary binary inputs, including empty input, inputs with long runs, inputs > 1 MiB, and highly random inputs).
- Implement LZ77 matching with a hash chain (window 32768, max match 258, min match 3) plus Huffman coding. Use **dynamic Huffman blocks** (BTYPE=10) with proper code-length-code encoding (RLE symbols 16/17/18), and fall back to **stored blocks** (BTYPE=00) whenever a dynamic block would be larger than the stored form for that chunk. A correct fixed-Huffman path is also acceptable as an internal fallback but dynamic must be the primary path so compression ratio is decent (roughly comparable to zlib level 6 on text).
- Bit-level writer must follow DEFLATE conventions exactly (LSB-first for Huffman/length bits, and the stored-block LEN/NLEN little-endian after byte alignment).
- No `unsafe`. No panics on any input.

## 2. `/workspace/src/zipfile.rs`
A minimal ZIP archive writer producing byte-level output that matches this observed layout:
- Local file headers with no data descriptor (flag_bits = 0), CRC/sizes known up front.
- Per entry: `version_made_by` and `version_needed`:
  - stored entries: 10 (both)
  - deflated entries: 20 (both)
- `create_system` = 3 (Unix) i.e. the high byte of version_made_by in the central directory is 3, so the central-directory "version made by" field is `0x0300 | version` (e.g. `0x030A` for stored, `0x0314` for deflated). Note: in the local header the version-needed field is just 10 or 20.
- `external_attr` = 0x81A40000 (regular file, mode 0644).
- `internal_attr` = 0.
- No extra field, no file comment, no archive comment.
- DOS date/time encoded from a supplied `(year, month, day, hour, minute, second)`.
- Central directory then End Of Central Directory record.

Public API:
```rust
pub struct ZipWriter {
    // ...
}

/// (year, month, day, hour, min, sec) — DOS timestamp applied to every entry.
pub type ZipTime = (u16, u8, u8, u8, u8, u8);

impl ZipWriter {
    pub fn new(time: ZipTime) -> Self;
    /// Add an entry compressed with DEFLATE.
    pub fn add_deflated(&mut self, name: &str, data: &[u8]);
    /// Add an entry stored uncompressed.
    pub fn add_stored(&mut self, name: &str, data: &[u8]);
    /// Finish and return the whole archive.
    pub fn finish(self) -> Vec<u8>;
}
```
Entries must appear in the archive in insertion order.

## Verification you must do before finishing
Write a temporary standalone test (e.g. `/tmp/ziptest/`) — a small Rust bin that `include!`s or copies the two modules — build it with `cargo build --offline`, produce archives, and verify with python3:
- `python3 -c "import zipfile; z=zipfile.ZipFile(p); print(z.testzip()); ..."` returns None (no corrupt entries) and every entry's bytes round-trip exactly.
- Check `infolist()` fields match the spec above (compress_type, extract_version, create_version, create_system, external_attr, flag_bits, extra==b'').
- Fuzz `deflate` against `zlib.decompress(data, -15)` on at least 300 random/structured inputs of varying sizes (0..2_000_000 bytes) — all must round-trip.
- Report the compression ratio you achieve on a ~100 KB text input versus python `zlib.compress(data,6)` size.

Do NOT create or modify any other file in /workspace besides these two `src/*.rs` files (in particular do not touch src/main.rs or Cargo.toml — another engineer is editing those concurrently). Clean up your /tmp scratch dir at the end is not required.

Finally report: the exact public API you exposed, verification results (round-trip counts, ratio numbers), and anything a caller must know.
